WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:06.300
After four seasons and innumerable betrayals,&nbsp;
the succeeding CEO of Waystar RoyCo has finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.384
been revealed. But could the end of the show&nbsp;
have been under our noses the whole time?

00:00:10.384 --> 00:00:14.940
Since Succession began, the question on viewers'&nbsp;
minds is who would ultimately carry on the Waystar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:21.000
RoyCo legacy after family patriarch Logan Roy&nbsp;
inevitably had to step down. Turns out, the mighty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.940
CEO stepped down sooner than anticipated, as Logan&nbsp;
died in the third episode of Season 4. Since that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:31.629
point, it's been a mad dash to acquire power&nbsp;
among the Roy siblings, and the series finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.629 --> 00:00:35.588
"With Open Eyes," finally brought things&nbsp;
to a close the only way it was possible.

00:00:35.588 --> 00:00:40.620
Tom Wambsgans ultimately receives an offer&nbsp;
to become CEO, which the Roy siblings try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:45.420
circumnavigate. However, at the last second,&nbsp;
Tom's wife, Shiv, sides with her husband — or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:50.454
against her brother Kendall, depending on how you&nbsp;
look at it — allowing Tom to acquire the position.

00:00:50.454 --> 00:00:54.240
Throughout the run of the show, there were&nbsp;
various fan theories suggesting Tom would end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.980
victorious over the Roy children. After all, Logan&nbsp;
appeared to take a real shine to him throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:04.260
the series. He even calls Tom "son" at one point,&nbsp;
albeit at a time when he was not entirely lucid.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.220
However, one theory that came out just&nbsp;
before the finale aired suggests the answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.255
was right before our eyes the whole time,&nbsp;
whether the showrunners realized it or not.

00:01:12.255 --> 00:01:16.620
Names in prestigious TV shows tend to&nbsp;
carry additional meaning. In Succession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:21.060
the last name "Roy" literally translates to&nbsp;
"King," so it's easy to see the symbolism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.020
there. Additionally, Tom's wife goes&nbsp;
by the nickname, Shiv, meaning knife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.585
which is appropriate for someone who's not&nbsp;
above using underhandedness to get her way.

00:01:29.585 --> 00:01:33.780
Even in the series finale, one could argue&nbsp;
she stabbed her brother in the back so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.460
her husband would become CEO. However, the last&nbsp;
name Wambsgans should've told everyone what to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:43.560
expect from the moment he was introduced. Sophie&nbsp;
Kihm, the editor-in-chief at Nameberry, posted a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.434
TikTok a few days before the finale offering a&nbsp;
significant hint why Tom would be the new CEO.

00:01:48.434 --> 00:01:51.761
Working for Nameberry, she naturally&nbsp;
points to how names have meaning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.761 --> 00:01:55.740
and while "Wambsgans" isn't significant&nbsp;
on its own, it does tie Tom to someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:00.480
who would mirror his own arc — baseball player&nbsp;
Bill Wambsganss. While the real-life athlete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:04.560
is spelled slightly different from the&nbsp;
erratic head of ATN, with two esses at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.334
the end instead of one, Bill did something&nbsp;
Tom would achieve in a different context.

00:02:08.334 --> 00:02:12.840
In the TikTok, Kihm explains that Wambsganss&nbsp;
was a baseball player best known for being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.860
the only player in history to complete&nbsp;
an unassisted triple play during a World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:21.960
Series game. In a baseball game, a triple play&nbsp;
occurs when all three outs are recorded on one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.330
single swing of the bat – it’s incredibly&nbsp;
rare to be done by one single fielder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:31.440
hence the term ‘unassisted triple play.’ It's&nbsp;
only been done 15 times throughout the sport's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.561
history, and Wambsganss did it during&nbsp;
baseball's equivalent of a series finale.

00:02:35.561 --> 00:02:39.560
In the Succession finale, Tom achieves&nbsp;
a similar feat by knocking all three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:42.180
Roy siblings out of contention for CEO at once.

00:02:42.433 --> 00:02:47.953
"Shiv, you should probably know: it's me."

00:02:47.953 --> 00:02:51.060
He even bested Kendall's attempt&nbsp;
to subvert his ascension,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:55.440
with the final vote coming down to Shiv, who&nbsp;
may or may not have considered Tom to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.270
better CEO than her brother — either&nbsp;
way, she had to stop Kendall's rise.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:03.900
Tom's journey to the top spot can be charted&nbsp;
back to the Season 3 finale, when he ratted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.341
out all three Roy siblings to Logan, making&nbsp;
a play that cut all three out of the running.

00:03:08.341 --> 00:03:12.000
As Kihm explains, it's important to&nbsp;
make a note of unusual surnames in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.540
film and television. "Wambsgans"&nbsp;
always stood out, but even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.680
it matches his character arc perfectly,&nbsp;
executive producer Frank Rich told Slate,

00:03:19.806 --> 00:03:21.606
"I hate to spoil the internet's fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.606 --> 00:03:25.534
but it's false [...] Tom's family name was&nbsp;
picked before we had shot a first season."

00:03:25.534 --> 00:03:29.100
He went on to mention how many writers&nbsp;
for the show are British and therefore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.459
likely wouldn't be up-to-date with&nbsp;
obscure American baseball names.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:36.255
It just so happens to be one of the&nbsp;
biggest coincidences in TV history.

